 "summaryEn": "<p>This poem by <b>Vyloppilli Sreedhara Menon</b> calls out to young people: <b>come and carry these torches!</b> The torches have been carried by many, many generations before.</p><p>Long ago, in the middle of the forest, our ancestors' thoughts \"rubbed together\" (like stones making sparks), and a <b>flame</b> was born. The darkness got scared, and people saw a promise of a better world in the fire's smile.</p><p>Crowds of young people carried thousands of torches down the long road of time. With fire they cleared land for golden crops, melted iron to make tools, lit the lamps of knowledge, and warmed up the arts. The torches lifted the human spirit and made beautiful <b>new dawns</b> in the darkness. Big idea: fire stands for knowledge, and now it is your turn to carry it.</p>",
